(((window.HALCYON_DATA=window.HALCYON_DATA||{}).asep=window.HALCYON_DATA.asep||{}).full=window.HALCYON_DATA.asep.full||[]).push({
  num: 9, part: `Part Two: Development`, title: `Pueblo`, weeks: `Weeks 21 to 24`,
  tasks: [`M1`,`M4`,`T6`,`T12`,`T13`,`X1`,`X2`,`X3`,`X4`],
  cast: [],
  opening: [
    `Wes was leaning on her car when she came out, his coat over one arm, with the sun still well over the foothills. CDR had closed forty minutes ago. The hatch had read nine oh six twice.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Wes">"Monday Odette starts the hazard reports for the safety panel,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Wes">"I want one line in the tailoring before she does. What flew on the cargo modules flies here as built. The shell, the latches, the relief valves. Eleven modules. I'm not paying to prove it twice."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"The cargo modules didn't have anybody in them."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Wes">"The air didn't know that."</span> She told him she'd have an answer Monday. He tapped the roof of her car twice and walked back to his own truck.`,
    `She drove south with the radio off. Past Castle Rock the traffic thinned. Past Colorado Springs, where her old shop's lights still showed from the highway, there was only the road and the dark line of the mountains on her right. Two hours. It used to be one.`,
    `Her father's porch light was on. He was in his chair with the ballgame on and the sound down, and he got up when she came in as if he hadn't been watching the window since eight. <span class="said" data-who="Frank">"You eat?"</span> She hadn't. He made her eggs with his right hand on the pan and his left in the pocket of his cardigan.`,
    `Callie came Saturday at five with Rosie and a bowl of potato salad under foil, in Mom's good yellow bowl. Rosie ran right past Nadia to the fridge. The purple rocket was up there under a trout magnet from some bait shop in Salida, and Rosie jabbed a finger at the back of it, where a door had been added in green crayon. The handle was bigger than the door. That one was Nadia's, she said. Mommy said. Nadia asked if it opened. Both ways, Rosie said, obviously, and took off yelling for Grandpa.`,
    `First time all three of them at that table since Christmas. Mom's chair was still on the end by the window. Rosie knelt on it so she could reach.`,
    `Her dad had shoved his sleeves up to cut tomatoes. Store ones, two ninety nine a pound, which he called robbery, because his own were knee high and green in their wire cages out back. In the kitchen light the whole arm showed. The scar ran from the wrist most of the way to the elbow, pale and shiny, skin pulled tight like candle wax that ran and set. His left hand sat on the oilcloth half closed. The fingers quit where they always quit, short of a fist. She'd grown up seeing that hand a piece at a time. A cuff. A work glove. A jacket pocket in Tiago's high bay, the whole tour. She had never seen him just leave it out on a table.`,
    `Rosie hooked her finger in the curl of his like it was a mug handle. He lifted. Her hand came up an inch off the table and she shrieked, so he set it down, so she made him do it again. Five times. To Rosie that was what Grandpa's hand was for.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Frank">"So,"</span> he said, around a bite of bratwurst. <span class="said" data-who="Frank">"They pass it?"</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"It passed."</span> Then the rest, the way she'd rehearsed it somewhere around Fountain. The hatch hanging off the crane. Nine oh six, against eight fifty. Margin down to about 810 kilograms. Monday the hazard reports started, and anything that could kill the crew had to be two fault tolerant.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Frank">"Which is what."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"Any two things fail, the crew's still okay. Takes three."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Frank">"Three what."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"Controls. Three separate things, and any one of them stops it."</span>`,
    `He set his fork on the plate, tines down. <span class="said" data-who="Frank">"Separate how?"</span> Callie glanced at him, then at her potato salad, the way you look off when somebody's about to say a thing you know by heart.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Frank">"End of October,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Frank">"Ice storm, that year. Half the east side down. Third night. Ernie's crew opens the switch out on the county road, Ernie tests it. Ernie's good. Best I ever worked with. Dead. He calls it dead on the radio and I go a mile down to our pole."</span> He looked at the hand on the table like it was some guy he used to know. <span class="said" data-who="Frank">"They tested it at the switch, and it was dead at the switch."</span> He stopped long enough that Rosie looked up. <span class="said" data-who="Frank">"I wasn't at the switch."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"What was on it?"</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Frank">"Fella out on a farm road had a generator. Wanted his freezer running. Plugged it in through his dryer outlet and never threw his main, so it went back up through his transformer. Transformer don't care which way it goes. Two forty in, seventy two hundred out. Our side."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Frank">"Tester was in the truck,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Frank">"Grounds were in the truck. Ernie said dead and I was tired, and up I went."</span> He turned the hand over, palm up, as far as it'd go. <span class="said" data-who="Frank">"That's where it comes from. You don't climb a pole you haven't grounded. Well. I climbed one."</span>`,
    `Those words had been on her phone since her first morning at FRO, in capitals. She'd taken them for advice. It had never crossed her mind they were a confession.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Frank">"After, when I was teaching, they had to test it where they touch. Every time. Then grounds on both sides of the work, dead or not, I don't care what anybody said on the radio."</span> He tapped the oilcloth with one finger of his right hand, slow. <span class="said" data-who="Frank">"Switch is somebody else's check. Tester's yours. Grounds are for when you're both wrong."</span> He looked at her, not the table. <span class="said" data-who="Frank">"Don't hear that as one tester would've saved me. Maybe. Maybe not. One check is one check."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"Three things."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Frank">"Three different things. Ernie checked it right. Checked it in the wrong place, is all. And that fella's generator, that wasn't on Ernie."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"So whose was it?"</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Frank">"I'm the one that climbed."</span>`,
    `Rosie had gone quiet on Mom's chair, still holding his finger. Callie said it to the table. <span class="said" data-who="Callie">"He told it every class. First morning, before coffee. Every class he ever had."</span>`,
    `Nadia looked at her father. <span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"I didn't know that."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Callie">"I know you didn't."</span>`,
    `Rosie conked out against him at half past eight. He carried her to the couch and got the afghan over her one handed, the orange and brown one the whole family pretended to hate. From the hall he said goodnight. <span class="said" data-who="Frank">"Leave the dishes."</span> They didn't.`,
    `Callie washed and Nadia dried, and for a while there was only the water and the plates. Then Nadia said, <span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"You give that call."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Callie">"What call?"</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"Ernie's. Dead at the switch. That's your job now."</span>`,
    `Callie turned the water off. <span class="said" data-who="Callie">"Say what you mean."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"I mean you tell a crew a line is dead from a room thirty miles away, and they go up."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Callie">"They go up after they test it where they're touching and hang grounds on both sides,"</span> Callie said, <span class="said" data-who="Callie">"because he taught every one of them, or he taught the guys who taught them. You think I don't know whose story that is? I hear it every time I key the radio. That's why it's never one call. That's the whole thing he just told you."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"It was one call that night."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Callie">"And it's three now, because of that night. That's what he did with it."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"And the night somebody's tired and leaves the tester in the truck."</span>`,
    `Callie dropped the towel on the counter. <span class="said" data-who="Callie">"Then I've got a crew lead who knows I'll pull him off that pole, and a crew that won't let him up it. At some point you trust the check, Nadia. Or nobody climbs."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"At some point you trust the check, and that's the night somebody climbs."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Callie">"You heard it once. Tonight. I heard it at nineteen, second row, with eleven guys who didn't know I was his kid, and then I went up poles for four years. You don't get to drive down twice a year and tell me what it means."</span>`,
    `Four years. Nadia had always known the number. She had never once let herself picture it: Callie at the top of a pole in an ice storm, waiting on somebody's radio.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"I came down."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Callie">"You came down to tell him it passed. I drive him to his appointments. I know which Sundays he goes quiet."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"That's not fair."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Callie">"No,"</span> Callie said. <span class="said" data-who="Callie">"It's not."</span>`,
    `Rosie was in the doorway in one of Frank's T-shirts, which came to her ankles, dragging the afghan by a corner. She told them they were loud. Callie picked her up, afghan and all, and at the door said without turning around that Nadia should tell Dad she'd be by Monday. The truck's lights swung across the kitchen wall and were gone.`,
    `Nadia sat back down. Rosie had taken the rocket off the fridge at some point and left it on the table, the green door at the back. Her father's knife was still on the cutting board where he'd set it down to tell it.`,
    `In the morning he was out in the tomatoes before she was up, on one knee, tying vines to the cages with strips of an old T shirt. The right hand did the knots. The left held the stake.`,
    `She asked him why she'd never heard it. Not once, in twenty two years.`,
    `He pulled a knot tight. <span class="said" data-who="Frank">"You were nine. You'd have checked my truck for that tester every morning till you left home."</span> He moved down to the next cage. <span class="said" data-who="Frank">"You did anyway."</span>`
  ],
  lesson: {
    title: `Colleen's Flight Notes: two of everything`,
    sections: [
      { h: `Two magnetos`,
        body: [`She left him in the tomatoes and drove across town to the Pueblo airport. Colleen had said she'd been meaning to fly down to the museum there anyway, which Nadia didn't believe for a second. They walked the rows of old bombers for an hour. Nadia told her about the table, not all of it. Then Colleen walked her out to the blue and white airplane on the ramp and laid a hand on the cowling.`,
          `<span class="said" data-who="Colleen">"Two magnetos in there,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Colleen">"Two plugs in every cylinder, two separate ignition systems, and either one runs the engine. Before every takeoff I switch to left alone, then right alone, and watch the needle. If I only ever ran on both, one could be dead for a month and I wouldn't know until the other one quit."</span>`,
          `Reliability is the probability that a system performs its required function, under stated conditions, for a stated time. All three parts matter, and a number with no time attached says nothing. Mean time between failures, MTBF, is an average. At a constant failure rate about 63 percent of units fail before they reach it, so it's never a promised life. A reliability block diagram shows how parts combine. In series every block has to work, so you multiply their reliabilities. In parallel any one block is enough, so the group fails only if every branch fails: one minus the product of their failure probabilities.`,
          `<span class="said" data-who="Colleen">"Two magnetos,"</span> Colleen said, <span class="said" data-who="Colleen">"one crankshaft."</span> A single-point failure is any one item whose failure takes the function down. A common cause is one thing that takes out several supposedly independent parts at once: a shared power supply, a shared pipe, a shared procedure, the same bad lot. Redundancy only helps when the branches fail independently. A backup that's never tested by itself can sit failed for months, which is why each branch gets checked alone.`],
        terms: [[`Reliability`,`The probability that a system performs its required function under stated conditions for a stated period of time.`],[`Single-point failure`,`A failure of one item that, by itself, causes loss of a system function. Common cause failures defeat redundancy the same way.`]],
        exam: `In series, multiply reliabilities. In parallel, the group fails only if all branches fail. MTBF is an average, not a life. Redundancy counts only where branches are independent.` },
      { h: `Ready when it's needed`,
        body: [`Last summer Colleen's alternator quit at Erie. The mechanic changed it in forty minutes. The part took eleven days to come from Wichita. <span class="said" data-who="Colleen">"The airplane was broken for forty minutes,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Colleen">"It was grounded for eleven days."</span>`,
          `Availability is the probability that a system is ready to work when it's called on. Inherent availability counts only failure and repair: MTBF divided by MTBF plus mean time to repair. Operational availability counts what the user lives with, including the wait for parts, people and tools. On orbit that wait can be a supply flight months away, so spares and logistics decide availability as much as the part does.`,
          `Maintainability is how quickly and easily the people and tools that will really be there can keep a system working or restore it. Crewed spacecraft are built around orbital replacement units, ORUs, chosen for their failure rate and how critical they are, and designed so a crew can swap them with the tools on board. The scarcest thing on orbit is crew time, so maintenance hours get a budget, like mass. The maintenance process plans and does that work. Logistics keeps the spares where they're needed. Affordability asks what the system costs over its whole life, and sustainability asks what that life takes from the environment and the people around it. Interoperability asks whether it works with the systems it has to talk to, resilience whether it keeps going when something breaks, and testability whether a fault can be found and pinned down.`],
        terms: [[`Availability`,`The probability that a system is operable when needed. Inherent availability is MTBF over MTBF plus MTTR; operational availability adds real delays.`],[`Orbital replacement unit (ORU)`,`A unit designed to be removed and replaced on orbit by the crew, chosen for failure rate and criticality.`]],
        exam: `Inherent availability counts repair time only. Operational availability adds logistics and waiting. Maintainability is designed in, and crew time is a budget.` },
      { h: `A promise to a person`,
        body: [`On the way back to the car Colleen asked what Nadia's father had done after he got hurt. Trained crews, Nadia said, every year until he retired. <span class="said" data-who="Colleen">"Then he wrote hazard reports,"</span> Colleen said. <span class="said" data-who="Colleen">"He just said them out loud."</span>`,
          `Safety engineering finds hazards, works out their causes and effects, and controls them. On crewed space programs each hazard gets a hazard report: the hazard, every cause, the controls for each cause, and how each control will be verified. Some hazards, like a pressure shell bursting, can't be handled by counting failures; they're controlled by design for minimum risk, with large structural margins and proof testing. For catastrophic hazards the usual rule is two fault tolerance: no combination of two failures or operator errors can lead to the catastrophe. That takes three controls, and a control counts only if it can stop the hazard by itself, shares no cause with the others, and is verified on its own. Three parts behind one shared part are one control. A safety panel reviews the reports in phases that follow the design, and the last phase, before delivery, checks that every control's verification is closed.`,
          `<span class="said" data-who="Colleen">"A hazard report is a promise to a person,"</span> Colleen said. <span class="said" data-who="Colleen">"Somebody you'll never meet is going to sleep on it."</span>`],
        terms: [[`Hazard report`,`A record of a hazard, its causes, the controls for each cause, and how each control is verified.`],[`Two fault tolerance`,`No combination of two failures or operator errors can result in the hazard. Requires independent controls.`]],
        exam: `Two fault tolerance: no two failures or errors cause the catastrophe. Controls must be independent and each verified. Severity comes from the worst credible outcome.` },
      { h: `Same work, other rules`,
        body: [`Nadia told her about Callie's control room: switching orders, clearances, the crew that tests and grounds anyway. Colleen nodded the whole way through. <span class="said" data-who="Colleen">"Same processes,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Colleen">"Different rulebook."</span>`,
          `The handbook applies systems engineering across system types, such as physical products, software intensive systems, services and enterprises, and across domains, such as space, power and energy, healthcare and transportation. The processes stay the same. Each domain brings its own regulators, standards and risks, and each type changes what matters most. A crewed habitat isn't a cargo module with people added. With a person inside, safety, maintainability and human systems integration move to the front, and a rule that was fine for cargo has to earn its place again. Tailoring fits the processes to the type, domain and size on purpose, with the reasons written down and agreed by whoever imposed the requirement. It changes how the work gets done. It doesn't remove what protects people.`,
          `System security protects a system's function from threats, such as a command that didn't come from who it claims: authenticate and authorize every command, give each user only the access they need, layer the defenses, and design it in from the requirements. Human systems integration treats the people as part of the system, crew and ground controllers alike, and designs displays, alarms, procedures, workload and training around them. The operation process puts it all to use: who runs the system, who acts when it fails, and how ground and crew hand off.`],
        terms: [[`System types and domains`,`The kinds of system (product, software intensive, service, enterprise) and the industries they serve. The processes stay the same; the rules and priorities change.`],[`Human systems integration (HSI)`,`Treating the people who operate and maintain a system as part of it, and designing for their tasks, workload and training.`]],
        exam: `Same SE processes across types and domains, tailored to each. Tailoring keeps safety requirements. Security is designed in. HSI covers operators and maintainers, not just users.` }
    ]
  },
  scenes: [
    { id:`f9s1`, domain:`Cross-cutting`, task:`X4`, title:`Eleven modules`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Monday morning Wes's line was already in the tailoring draft, in his words, typed in himself: hardware with cargo flight heritage is accepted as built, no new analysis. Odette had the list of forty hazard reports for the phase two safety review in week twenty six. Nine of them leaned on cargo heritage, from the hatch latches to the cabin relief valves.`,`Nadia had driven back Sunday afternoon. Callie's truck never came down the street before she left.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Wes">"Eleven modules,"</span> Wes said from the doorway. <span class="said" data-who="Wes">"Not one squawk on orbit."</span> He didn't mention the week before one of them shipped, and neither did she.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Check each of the nine against the new conditions, a crew aboard for fifteen years.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`She and Odette go down the nine that afternoon. For each one they write what flew, under what conditions, and what's different now: people inside, fifteen years instead of weeks, two fault tolerance, a hatch that opens hundreds of times. Where the conditions match, the cargo evidence counts. Where they don't, the item gets new analysis and a date. Wes reads the list standing up and doesn't strike anything. The relief valves come last, marked low: passive, never opened in flight. <span class="said" data-who="Wes">"Never opened because nothing ever went wrong,"</span> he says, and Odette writes that down too.`,
          why:`A crewed habitat is a different type of system from an uncrewed cargo module. Heritage evidence carries over only where the conditions match, so each reused item is checked against the new system's hazards, life and environment.`},
        {t:`Accept Wes's rule as written with the eleven flight records cited in all nine reports.`, s:0, d:{trust:4,conf:-2,health:-5},
          after:`The line stays in. Nine hazard reports now cite cargo flights that carried no crew, for weeks rather than fifteen years, and the safety panel will be first to ask why.`,
          why:`Flight history is evidence for the conditions it came from. Carrying a rule from uncrewed cargo into a crewed habitat skips the question of what the new system type changes.`},
        {t:`Keep the rule for now, and review all nine against crewed use before the module ships.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`The rule goes in with a promise behind it. The review lands in the months the flight hardware is being built, when anything it finds costs the most to change.`,
          why:`Reviewing heritage is right. Doing it at the end turns design questions into rework.`},
        {t:`Drop heritage credit and requalify every cargo part as new for the crewed module.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`Amara prices it at five months. Wes asks if they'll be requalifying aluminum too. The plan goes back in a drawer, and the nine items go in with no review at all.`,
          why:`Reuse with evidence is good engineering. Tailoring for a new system type means checking what changed, not throwing out what's known.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f9s2`, domain:`Cross-cutting`, task:`X2`, title:`Twenty thousand hours`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tuesday Jun brought Tamarack's data sheet for the rack's main fan to Nadia's desk, with Halo's requirement highlighted beside it: ventilation MTBF of at least 20,000 hours. Tamarack rated the fan at 20,000.`,`Amara had already seen it. <span class="said" data-who="Amara">"Twenty thousand hours is two years and change,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Amara">"A mission's six months. We're covered four times over."</span>`,`Jun didn't say anything, which from Jun meant he thought she was wrong and wanted Nadia to be the one to say it.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Close the requirement on Tamarack's rating of 20,000 hours against a 4,320 hour mission.`, s:0, d:{trust:4,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`The requirement closes on paper. About one mission in five, the fan quits.`,
          why:`MTBF isn't a guaranteed life. At a constant failure rate about 63 percent of units fail before the MTBF, so comparing it to mission length overstates reliability.`},
        {t:`Ask Tamarack for the test data behind the 20,000 hour rating before closing it out.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`Tamarack sends good data, and the 20,000 holds up. It still says nothing about the odds of losing the fan on a given mission.`,
          why:`Checking the evidence is right. The measure still has to be turned into a probability over the mission before it tells you what the crew can count on.`},
        {t:`Restate the need as the chance one fan runs a full 180 day mission without failing.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`Jun works it at a constant failure rate. A 180 day mission is 4,320 hours, and the chance one fan runs the whole mission without failing comes out about 81 percent. Roughly one mission in five loses it. Nadia rewrites the requirement as the probability of keeping cabin air moving through a mission, with the conditions stated, and asks Jun for a block diagram of everything the air depends on.`,
          why:`Reliability is the probability of performing a function under stated conditions for a stated time. MTBF is an average, not a life. Stating reliability over the mission shows what the crew can actually count on.`},
        {t:`Ask Tamarack to quote a fan rated at 40,000 hours to double the margin over a mission.`, s:0, d:{trust:2,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`Tamarack quotes a new fan design and nine months. The requirement still compares an average with a mission length.`,
          why:`A tighter number on a misread measure costs money without answering the question. Define reliability for the mission first.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f9s3`, domain:`Cross-cutting`, task:`X2`, title:`Ninety days`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const pad=G&&G.paddedmargin;return [`Wednesday Helen sent Halo's availability requirement for CO2 removal: ready 99 percent of the time. Pavel answered the same afternoon. The rack's controller card had an MTBF of 8,000 hours, and a crew could swap it in four. Eight thousand over 8,004, he wrote. 99.95 percent.`,`There was no spare card on board in the plan. The nearest one would be on the ground waiting for a supply flight, and Halo's flights ran every ninety days.`,
        (pad
          ?`A spare card weighed nine kilograms. Halo believed HEARTH had seventeen hundred kilograms of margin to spend. Nadia had done it again on the drive back from Pueblo: about 810, sixty over the floor.`
          :`A spare card weighed nine kilograms, and every kilogram now came out of a margin of about 810, sixty over the floor.`)]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Send Pavel's 99.95 percent to Halo with his MTBF plus the four hour swap time.`, s:0, d:{trust:4,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`Halo files 99.95. The first time the card fails, the crew waits a month and a half for a new one.`,
          why:`That figure leaves out the logistics delay. Availability for a decision has to count every source of downtime the user will see.`},
        {t:`Add the average wait for a spare card to fly up to the downtime in Pavel's figure.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`Jun runs it with no spare aboard: four hours to swap, plus an average of forty five days waiting for a card to fly up. That's 1,084 hours down for every 8,000 up, about 88 percent. With a spare in the rack's own stowage it's back above 99.9. Nadia sends Helen both numbers and the nine kilograms. Helen takes the spare and asks what else on the list looks like that.`,
          why:`Inherent availability counts only failure and repair. Operational availability adds the delays the user really lives with, such as waiting for a spare. On orbit, logistics decide availability as much as the part does.`},
        {t:`Label Pavel's figure as inherent availability, for Halo's logistics team to finish.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`The label is honest. Helen reads 99.95 anyway, and the spare stays on the ground.`,
          why:`Calling it inherent is accurate, but the supplier still owns showing what the number means for the user. Spares and logistics belong in the availability case.`},
        {t:`Ask Tamarack to raise the card's MTBF until a spare is unlikely to be needed.`, s:0, d:{trust:2,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`Pavel says the card is already good for its class. The wait for a supply flight is the same either way.`,
          why:`Better reliability helps, but here the downtime comes from logistics delay. Work on the biggest contributor.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f9s4`, domain:`Technical`, task:`T13`, title:`Behind the card`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const blamed=G&&G.blamedsupplier;return [
        (blamed
          ?`Thursday Pavel came down from Frederick with the rack's maintenance drawing and his contracts manager.`
          :`Thursday Pavel brought the rack's maintenance drawing down from Frederick himself.`),
        `The main fan sat behind the 120 volt card Tamarack had added in week twelve. To reach it, a crew member would have to pull the card first: twenty two fasteners, two tools that weren't in the station's kit, about six hours.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Pavel">"On the old station the integrator decided what the crew could swap,"</span> Pavel said. <span class="said" data-who="Pavel">"We never had to."</span>`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Keep the layout as drawn, with six hours of crew time booked for each fan change.`, s:0, d:{trust:3,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`The fan fails in month four. The crew spends a day on it, and the rack is down the whole time.`,
          why:`Crew time and downtime are what maintainability is measured in. A likely failure that takes six hours and special tools isn't maintainable on orbit.`},
        {t:`Have Tamarack write a crew procedure for the fan change, with photos at each step.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`The procedure is thorough. It's thorough about a six hour job with two tools the crew doesn't have.`,
          why:`Good procedures help, but they can't make a hard design easy to maintain. Access and tools are design choices.`},
        {t:`Ask Halo to add Tamarack's two tools to the station kit, with training for the crew.`, s:0, d:{trust:2,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`Halo says the kit is fixed across the station, and every new tool is mass in every module. The fan is still behind the card.`,
          why:`Changing the station to suit one module's design pushes the cost onto everyone. Design the unit for the support that exists.`},
        {t:`Pick the units the crew will replace by failure rate, then design each to the kit.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`They sit at the drawing all afternoon. Jun lists every part with its failure rate. Four become ORUs: the fan, the controller card, the CO2 sorbent cartridges and the power card. Each has to come out from the front face, with captive fasteners, using tools already in the station's kit, in under an hour. The fan moves in front of the card. Tamarack says two weeks, inside its float. Pavel says Irena, on the frame bench, drew it that way in March, and he'd told her not to bother.`,
          why:`Maintainability is designed in. Orbital replacement units are chosen for failure rate and criticality, then designed so a crew can swap them with the tools and time they'll have on board.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f9s5`, domain:`Technical`, task:`T13`, title:`Hours in a week`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const p7=(all&&all[7]&&all[7].picks)||{};return [`Friday Helen's crew office sent its maintenance budget: three crew hours a week for HEARTH, scheduled work only. Jun's list of HEARTH's scheduled tasks came to five and a half. Cabin filters, the hygiene station, water samples, sorbent cartridges, inspections.`+(p7[8]===1?` One of them was the ten minute hatch seal check Helen had asked for in writing in week seventeen.`:``),`Helen's note had one more line. The crew was up there to do work Halo's customers paid for, and every maintenance hour came out of it.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Jun">"Do I just send them the five and a half?"</span> Jun asked.`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Budget crew time like mass and design down the largest tasks on Jun's list.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`Crew time goes on the measures list as its own line, with a budget and a trend. The biggest task is cleaning the cabin filters every week, an hour and a half. A bigger filter, changed monthly, cuts that to twenty minutes a week. Water sampling, eighty six minutes a week, goes to an inline monitor the ground can read. The list comes to two point nine hours. Helen asks to see the line every month.`,
          why:`Maintenance planning counts the people and time it needs. On orbit crew time is the scarcest resource, so it's budgeted and tracked, and design changes go where they save the most.`},
        {t:`Ask Halo to raise HEARTH's crew time to match Jun's list of scheduled tasks.`, s:0, d:{trust:3,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`Halo says no. Every hour it gives HEARTH comes out of the work its customers pay for.`,
          why:`The maintenance concept has to fit the support that exists. Asking for more of the scarcest resource is the last resort, not the first.`},
        {t:`Hand every task the ground can watch to ground monitoring, and keep the rest.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`Water sampling and two inspections go to the ground. The cabin filters, the biggest task, stay exactly as they were.`,
          why:`Ground monitoring helps. Without a budget to track against, the largest tasks don't get designed down.`},
        {t:`Stretch each task's interval until the total fits inside the three hours.`, s:0, d:{trust:3,conf:-1,health:-4},
          after:`The total fits. Filters cleaned every two weeks clog in ten days, and the fans work harder all mission.`,
          why:`Intervals come from how parts wear, not from the budget. Change the design, not just the schedule.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f9s6`, domain:`Technical`, task:`T6`, title:`Two fans, one box`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Sunday night Nadia had called at eight. She'd had her first line ready since Thursday. Rosie answered. When Callie came on it was to say Dad was fine and the tomatoes were fine, and then she handed the phone back to Rosie.`,`Monday of week twenty three Jun put his block diagram for the cabin air loop on the HEARTH room screen. Two cabin fans side by side, in parallel, so either one could keep the air moving. Both ran through one fan controller.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Cabin air loop, reliability block summary, one 180 day mission">Fan A: 0.90. Fan B: 0.90. In parallel.<br>Fan controller, shared by both fans: 0.98. In series with the pair.<br>Loop reliability: 0.9702.<br>Requirement: 0.98.</prop>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Wes">"Two fans,"</span> Wes said. <span class="said" data-who="Wes">"We doubled it."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Add a third fan in parallel with the pair, on the same shared controller.`, s:0, d:{trust:3,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`Three fans in parallel come to 0.999. Times the controller's 0.98, the loop is 0.979. Still short, and three fans still stop when one box does.`,
          why:`Adding redundancy behind a shared series element barely moves the total. The single point sets the ceiling.`},
        {t:`Buy a better controller rated 0.995, with both fans still running through it.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`0.99 times 0.995 is 0.985, which meets the number. One box still decides whether either fan runs.`,
          why:`A better series part raises the number, but it leaves the single-point failure in place. Meeting the figure and removing the single point aren't the same.`},
        {t:`Give each fan its own controller, two strings side by side in parallel.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`Each fan and its own controller make a string: 0.90 times 0.98 is 0.882. Two strings in parallel fail only if both do, so the loop comes to about 0.986. It meets the requirement, and no single box sits in the path. The second controller weighs three kilograms. Wes is quiet through the arithmetic. <span class="said" data-who="Wes">"The box was the weak link,"</span> he says. <span class="said" data-who="Wes">"Not the fans."</span>`,
          why:`In series, reliabilities multiply. In parallel, the group fails only if every branch fails. A shared element in series caps what any redundancy behind it can give, so independent strings beat more of the redundant part.`},
        {t:`Ask Halo to accept 0.97, with the fans' redundancy noted as the rationale.`, s:0, d:{trust:3,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`Halo sends it back in a day. The requirement says 0.98, and the shared controller is still a single point.`,
          why:`Redundancy only counts where the branches are independent. A shared controller in series takes both fans with it.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f9s7`, domain:`Cross-cutting`, task:`X2`, title:`Separate how`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const hid=G&&G.hidrisk;return [`Tuesday Odette brought the first hazard report up to the HEARTH room: carbon dioxide buildup, catastrophic. Too much CO2 and a crew gets headaches, then slow, then wrong about things.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Hazard report HR-07, CO2 buildup, controls (draft)">Hazard: CO2 builds up in HEARTH's cabin. Severity: catastrophic. Two fault tolerance required.<br>Control 1: rack CO2 removal. Verified by rack acceptance test.<br>Control 2: station CO2 removal through intermodule ventilation. Verified by station analysis.<br>Control 3: crew recognizes symptoms and leaves the module. Verified by crew training.</prop>`,
        (hid
          ?`Since the seal, Amara read every hazard report herself before it went to Halo, and she was reading this one over Odette's shoulder.`
          :`Three controls, three verifications. It looked done.`),
        `Down the list, the report for losing hatch isolation still pointed at the seal. The new lot's routine result had come back at 15 percent. Its fifteen year aging result was due in week twenty eight.`,
        `Nadia heard her father's question before she'd finished the third line. Separate how?`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Send the report on to Halo with its three controls, each verified as listed.`, s:0, d:{trust:3,conf:-2,health:-5},
          after:`The report goes to Halo with three controls. One of them depends on a crew that's already impaired.`,
          why:`Counting lines isn't fault tolerance. A control has to work on its own when it's needed, and a symptom that arrives with the hazard doesn't.`},
        {t:`Add portable CO2 monitors as a fourth control, keeping the other three.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`Four lines now. The third one still depends on the crew noticing, and it still counts.`,
          why:`More controls don't fix a control that doesn't work. Check each one for independence and real effect.`},
        {t:`Ask Odette to look again at the severity, critical rather than catastrophic.`, s:0, d:{trust:2,conf:-2,health:-5},
          after:`Odette looks again and doesn't move it. CO2 kills, and severity comes from the outcome, not from the controls on hand.`,
          why:`Severity is set by the worst credible outcome. Lowering it to fit the controls turns the analysis upside down.`},
        {t:`Check that each control works alone, with no cause shared by any other.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`The third line goes first. By the time a crew feels CO2, the judgment it takes to leave is already going, so symptoms aren't a control. Odette swaps in portable CO2 monitors with their own batteries and alarms. Then they ask of every pair what one thing could stop both. The intermodule fan runs on station power, not HEARTH's, and the monitors run on themselves. Nothing shared. Each control gets its own verification. Nadia asks for the same pass on all forty. Odette says that's two weeks, and takes half.`,
          why:`Two fault tolerance means no two failures or operator errors cause the catastrophe. Controls count only if each can prevent the hazard alone, shares no common cause with the others, and is verified independently.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f9s8`, domain:`Cross-cutting`, task:`X2`, title:`Who's asking`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const p6=(all&&all[6]&&all[6].picks)||{};return [
        (p6[7]===1
          ?`Halo's controllers had asked again in week twenty for ground commanding of the rack, and this time it went through as a priced change.`
          :`The ground commanding Halo had asked for on the rack was in this sprint.`),
        `Wednesday FRO's flight software lead brought her plan to the HEARTH room. The rack would take mode commands from Halo's ground through the station's network. Anything arriving on that network, she said, came from Halo.`,`<span class="said" data-who="woman">"Only Halo can reach the station network,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="woman">"If somebody else is on it, we've got bigger problems."</span>`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Accept commands from the station network, behind Halo's own network security.`, s:0, d:{trust:3,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`The rack trusts anything on the network. The first time a station laptop picks up something it shouldn't, so does the rack.`,
          why:`A network boundary is one layer, not the whole defense. Each system checks who's commanding it and what they're allowed to do.`},
        {t:`Have HEARTH verify each command's signature and the sender's rights first.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`They put the threats on the whiteboard in an hour: a spoofed command, a replayed one, a controller's laptop that isn't only a controller's anymore. Halo's ground signs each command, and HEARTH checks it before the rack acts. The ground can change modes and read telemetry. It can't turn off the air with crew aboard unless the crew agrees. No command can disable the crew override on the panel, and every command is logged. Helen asks how her controllers will change the keys, and that goes into the ops concept.`,
          why:`System security protects the function against threats and is designed in from the requirements: authenticate and authorize commands, grant only the access each user needs, layer the defenses, and keep a local override that no remote command can defeat.`},
        {t:`Put a password on the rack's command link, shared with Halo's controllers.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`The password lives in a shared file at Halo. It never changes, and it unlocks every command, the dangerous ones included.`,
          why:`A shared secret is one weak layer with no limit on what it unlocks. Security also needs authorization by role and protection for hazardous commands.`},
        {t:`Remove ground commanding from the rack, keeping every command on the crew panel.`, s:0, d:{trust:2,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`Helen's controllers lose the commanding they planned on, and the crew gets woken for every mode change in its sleep period.`,
          why:`Removing a needed function to avoid a threat trades away the stakeholder's need. Secure the function instead.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f9s9`, domain:`Cross-cutting`, task:`X2`, title:`Three names`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Thursday Helen's crew office sent back the rack's displays from their first walkthrough. Tamarack's panel showed faults as codes: E-47. Halo's ground screens showed the same fault as ARS FLT 47. HEARTH's draft procedures called it a rack fan trip. Three names for one fan.`,`Helen had put one question at the top. <span class="said" data-who="Helen">"What does my crew do first, and how would they know?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Give each fault one shared name, then test the panel on Halo's trainers.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`Each fault gets one name, the same on the panel, the ground screens and every procedure. The panel says what it is and the first thing to do, in that order. Jun and Tiago sit two of Helen's trainers at a mock panel with a stopwatch and throw faults at them. On the first run one trainer goes looking for a code card that isn't there. By the fourth both have the first step done in under a minute. Training needs go on a list for Halo.`,
          why:`Human systems integration treats crew and controllers as part of the system. Displays, alarms, procedures and training are designed around their tasks and tested with representative users.`},
        {t:`Keep Tamarack's codes, and give the crew a card that translates each one.`, s:0, d:{trust:3,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`The card works on the ground. On orbit it's in a pouch on the wrong wall the first time the fan trips.`,
          why:`A translation aid adds a step and a lookup under stress. Design the display for the person using it.`},
        {t:`Rename the codes in plain words, and let each team write its own procedures.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`The words are clearer. The crew's checklist and the ground's now give different first steps for the same fault.`,
          why:`Plain words help, but crew and controllers act together. One shared vocabulary and coordinated procedures are part of HSI.`},
        {t:`Add the fault codes to the syllabus Halo's trainers already run for the crew.`, s:0, d:{trust:3,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`They do learn them, slowly. The first real fault takes eleven minutes to name.`,
          why:`Training can't fix a design that fights its users. HSI designs the system so the human part works too.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f9s10`, domain:`Technical`, task:`T12`, title:`Sleep period`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Friday Helen drove up from Halo to work the rack's operations concept with Nadia and Jun. The question on the whiteboard was short. The rack faults in the middle of the crew's sleep period. Who does what?`,`Halo's ground was staffed around the clock. The crew slept eight hours. FRO's sustaining engineers would be in Centennial, on call. And the aft node, still a watch item on Nadia's register, would change who could reach what once it berthed.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Have the crew handle every rack fault, with the ground in support.`, s:0, d:{trust:3,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`The crew gets woken for a filter warning on the second night. By week three they're sleeping through the tone.`,
          why:`Giving every task to the crew ignores workload and the ground's role. An operations concept divides the work by situation and capability.`},
        {t:`Give routine faults to the ground, with hard cases settled as they come.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`Routine faults go to the ground. The first hard case comes at a shift change, with the ground and the crew each sure it's the other's.`,
          why:`Deciding the hard cases in advance is the point of an operations concept. Situations it skips become arguments during the fault.`},
        {t:`Write a page on who acts in each fault case, from routine to crew safety.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`It takes the morning. In normal operations the ground watches the rack and changes its modes. A fault that doesn't threaten the air waits for the crew's morning, or the ground clears it. Anything that does threaten it wakes the crew, and on board the crew has the last word. FRO's on call engineer answers the ground within thirty minutes. Each handover gets a checklist, and key changes for the command link happen on the ground's day shift. Helen takes the page back to fit into the station's own.`,
          why:`The operation process needs a concept for how the system is run: who monitors, who commands, who decides in each situation, and how ground and crew hand off. Defining it during design lets the design support it.`},
        {t:`Leave the operations concept to Halo, as the station's operator.`, s:0, d:{trust:2,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`Halo writes procedures for a rack it doesn't know. They assume the fan can be reached in five minutes.`,
          why:`The supplier knows how its system behaves and fails. The operations concept is shared work, and the design has to support it.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f9s11`, domain:`Cross-cutting`, task:`X4`, title:`Six hundred pages`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const hid=G&&G.hidrisk;const p7=(all&&all[7]&&all[7].picks)||{};return [`Monday of week twenty four OCSC's crew certification standard sat on the HEARTH room table, six hundred pages written for government programs with government staffs. Amara had flagged forty two pages of document formats, reports and review steps FRO couldn't staff.`,`Ines had come up from quality with FRO's own procedures under her arm, because half of what OCSC asked for, FRO already did under another name. Nadia still had Amara's card in her badge holder`+(p7[11]===2?`, the board's three conditions on the back.`:`.`)+(hid?` After the seal, the board's chair asked to see anything FRO sent OCSC before it went.`:``),`<span class="said" data-who="Amara">"Tailor it,"</span> Amara said. <span class="said" data-who="Amara">"Hard."</span>`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Ask OCSC for a blanket waiver, given how the standard was written.`, s:0, d:{trust:3,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`OCSC answers in a week: the standard certifies the crew's safety, not the company's size. The waiver request goes in the file, and so does what it says about FRO.`,
          why:`A standard imposed for crew safety isn't optional because it's heavy. Tailor it with the authority. Don't ask it away.`},
        {t:`Comply with all six hundred pages as written, in OCSC's own formats.`, s:1, d:{trust:2,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`It's safe, and it's six people writing reports for a year. Three of the reports repeat FRO's own, line for line.`,
          why:`Following every line verbatim is the opposite mistake. Tailoring fits the process to the project without losing what matters.`},
        {t:`Tailor out what FRO can't verify in time, listed as open work for later.`, s:0, d:{trust:3,conf:-2,health:-5},
          after:`Two of the lines on the open list are verifications of hazard controls. OCSC's observer circles both.`,
          why:`Tailoring can't drop safety verification because it's hard to schedule. Crew safety controls stay, and so does their verification.`},
        {t:`Map each line to FRO's own procedures, with every change agreed by OCSC.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`Ines maps every line of the standard to FRO's own procedures in two days. Where FRO's document carries the same content, it stands in for OCSC's format. Two reports become one. Each change gets a reason. Nothing that touches the hazard controls, two fault tolerance or the safety reviews moves. OCSC's observer signs two hundred and one lines and asks questions on thirteen, and Amara gets back most of her forty two pages.`,
          why:`Tailoring adapts a process to the project's type, size and risk on purpose, with rationale agreed by the authority that imposed it. It changes how the work is done, not the requirements that protect people.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f9s12`, domain:`Cross-cutting`, task:`X1`, title:`Cabin to vacuum`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Wednesday the relief valves came up, last on the heritage list. Odette had the hazard report open: cabin overpressure, catastrophic. If the station's pressure control ever failed open and kept feeding air, the shell would be pushed past what it was built to hold.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Hazard report HR-22, cabin overpressure, controls">Control 1: station pressure control. Verified by station analysis.<br>Control 2: positive pressure relief valve A. Verified by crack and reseat test.<br>Control 3: positive pressure relief valve B. Verified by crack and reseat test.</prop>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Wes">"Cargo flew one valve,"</span> Wes said. <span class="said" data-who="Wes">"We put the second one in at PDR, for your two faults."</span>`,`Three different things. It read like three different things.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Trace the whole path from cabin air to vacuum, ground procedures included.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`She asks Tiago for the drawings and the ground procedures and starts at the cabin, the way her father would have started at the pole. Air into the bulkhead fitting. Into the line, through whatever sits in it, into the valves, out the vent. Then every procedure that touches the line on the ground. She takes it home Wednesday night and works it to the end.`,
          why:`Systems thinking looks at the whole path and its interactions, operations included, not each part alone. Shared elements and procedures only show up when the path is traced end to end.`},
        {t:`Pull both valves' crack and reseat test records, and check each against spec.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`Both records are clean. Each valve cracks at its pressure and reseats. The records say nothing about what sits between the valves and the cabin.`,
          why:`Verifying each control is necessary. It doesn't show whether the controls share anything on the way to the hazard.`},
        {t:`Close the report on the eleven cargo flights that carried this same valve.`, s:0, d:{trust:4,conf:-2,health:-5},
          after:`The report closes. The cargo modules flew one valve with no crew aboard, and this path has never been looked at whole.`,
          why:`Heritage covers the configuration and conditions that flew. A changed path in a new type of system has to be looked at as a whole.`},
        {t:`Ask Wes to sign the report as the designer who knows the path best.`, s:0, d:{trust:4,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`Wes signs it. His signature tells the panel who drew the path, not whether it works.`,
          why:`A designer's signature is accountability, not analysis. The path still needs tracing end to end.`}
      ]}
  ],
  drills: [
    { after:`f9s6`, title:`Strings and shared parts`, setup:[`Jun's cabin air loop has two fans in parallel, each with a reliability of 0.90 over one mission. Both run through one shared controller with a reliability of 0.98, in series with the pair. Halo requires 0.98 for the loop.`,`The fix gives each fan its own controller, also 0.98, so the loop becomes two separate strings in parallel. Work each step, and round only at the end.`],
      fields:[{label:`Two fans in parallel, by themselves`, answer:0.99, tol:0},{label:`Loop with the shared controller`, answer:0.9702, tol:0.0001},{label:`Chance the loop fails, shared controller`, answer:0.0298, tol:0.0001},{label:`Three fans in parallel with the shared controller`, answer:0.97902, tol:0.0001},{label:`One string: a fan and its own controller`, answer:0.882, tol:0.0001},{label:`Two strings in parallel`, answer:0.986076, tol:0.0001}],
      solution:[`Two fans in parallel: each fails with probability 1 minus 0.90 = 0.10. Both fail with 0.10 times 0.10 = 0.01. So the pair works with 1 minus 0.01 = 0.99.`,`Add the shared controller in series: 0.99 times 0.98 = 0.9702.`,`The loop fails with 1 minus 0.9702 = 0.0298, about three missions in a hundred.`,`A third fan: all three fail with 0.10 times 0.10 times 0.10 = 0.001, so the fans give 0.999. Times 0.98 is 0.97902. Still under 0.98. The shared controller sets the ceiling, and no number of fans behind it can get past 0.98.`,`One string, a fan and its own controller in series: 0.90 times 0.98 = 0.882.`,`Two strings in parallel: each fails with 1 minus 0.882 = 0.118. Both fail with 0.118 times 0.118 = 0.013924. So the loop works with 1 minus 0.013924 = 0.986076. That meets 0.98, and no single box can stop both fans.`] }
  ],
  exercise: { after:`f9s5`, title:`Which characteristic?`, intro:`Six of the handbook's quality characteristics showed up this week. Pick the one each HEARTH item is about.`,
    items:[
      {prompt:`The odds the rack's fan runs a whole 180 day mission without failing.`, choices:[`Availability`,`Reliability`,`Maintainability`,`Safety`], answer:1, why:`The probability of working, under stated conditions, for a stated time is reliability.`},
      {prompt:`The share of time CO2 removal is ready, counting the wait for a spare.`, choices:[`Availability`,`Reliability`,`System security`,`Maintainability`], answer:0, why:`Readiness when needed, including real delays, is operational availability.`},
      {prompt:`How fast a crew can swap the rack's fan using the station's own tools.`, choices:[`Safety`,`Human systems integration`,`Maintainability`,`Reliability`], answer:2, why:`How quickly and easily the system is restored by the people and tools on hand is maintainability.`},
      {prompt:`Commands to the rack are checked for who sent them before it acts.`, choices:[`Availability`,`Safety`,`Human systems integration`,`System security`], answer:3, why:`Protecting the command path from commands that aren't who they claim to be is system security.`},
      {prompt:`One name for each fault on the panel, the ground screens and every procedure.`, choices:[`Human systems integration`,`System security`,`Reliability`,`Availability`], answer:0, why:`Designing displays and procedures around crew and controllers is human systems integration.`},
      {prompt:`No two failures can leave the crew facing cabin overpressure.`, choices:[`Maintainability`,`Safety`,`Availability`,`Reliability`], answer:1, why:`Two fault tolerance for a catastrophic hazard is a safety requirement.`}
    ]},
  quiz: [
    {q:`What does reliability describe?`, opts:[`The chance a system works as required, under stated conditions, for a stated time`,`The share of total time a system is ready to use whenever its users call on it`,`How quickly a failed system can be restored by the people who maintain it`,`The average number of operating hours a system runs before being retired`], a:0, why:`Reliability is the probability of performing the required function under stated conditions for a stated time. Readiness is availability, and restoring is maintainability.`, task:`X2`, domain:`Cross-cutting`},
    {q:`A unit has an MTBF of 990 hours and a mean time to repair of 10 hours. What is its inherent availability?`, opts:[`0.90`,`0.999`,`0.99`,`0.10`], a:2, why:`Inherent availability is MTBF over MTBF plus MTTR: 990 divided by 1,000 is 0.99.`, task:`X2`, domain:`Cross-cutting`},
    {q:`For a catastrophic hazard, what does two fault tolerance require?`, opts:[`Two controls, each verified by its own test`,`Two separate reviews of every hazard report`,`A spare for each control that fails in service`,`No two failures or errors can cause the hazard`], a:3, why:`Two fault tolerance means no combination of two failures or operator errors leads to the hazard, which takes independent controls, each verified.`, task:`X2`, domain:`Cross-cutting`},
    {q:`A company that has built uncrewed cargo modules wins its first crewed habitat. How should it treat its cargo design rules?`, opts:[`Apply them unchanged, since the hardware family is the same`,`Recheck each one against what a crew aboard changes`,`Set them all aside and start again from first principles`,`Use them only where the customer has approved each one`], a:1, why:`A crewed habitat is a different system type. Rules and heritage carry over where the conditions match, after checking what the new type changes.`, task:`X4`, domain:`Cross-cutting`},
    {q:`A program must meet a heavy government standard. What makes its changes to that standard tailoring rather than noncompliance?`, opts:[`They're planned, justified, and agreed with the authority`,`They cut the program's cost and schedule by a meaningful amount`,`They were approved in writing by the program's own manager`,`They touch only the documents, and never the hardware`], a:0, why:`Tailoring is a deliberate, documented adaptation with a rationale, agreed by whoever imposed the requirement, and it keeps what protects people.`, task:`X4`, domain:`Cross-cutting`},
    {q:`How does the handbook treat systems engineering across domains such as power utilities and spaceflight?`, opts:[`Each domain needs its own, different set of processes`,`Only the heavily regulated domains need systems engineering`,`Domains only matter once the system enters operation`,`The same processes apply, adapted to each domain's rules`], a:3, why:`The processes are the same across domains and system types. Each domain brings its own regulations, standards and risks, and the processes are tailored to fit.`, task:`X4`, domain:`Cross-cutting`},
    {q:`On a crewed spacecraft, which parts are best made orbital replacement units?`, opts:[`Every single part, so the crew can repair anything at all`,`The heaviest parts, to keep each launch simpler`,`Likely or critical failures, designed for a crew swap`,`The parts the supplier finds easiest to build`], a:2, why:`ORUs are chosen for failure rate and criticality, then designed so the crew can replace them with the tools and time on board.`, task:`T13`, domain:`Technical`},
    {q:`A module's scheduled maintenance needs more crew hours than the operator allows. What's the best response?`, opts:[`Ask the operator for more hours, since the tasks are real`,`Track crew time against the budget and design out big tasks`,`Lengthen each task's interval until the total fits`,`Drop inspections that haven't found anything so far`], a:1, why:`Crew time is a budgeted resource. Track it and change the design where it saves the most, rather than stretching intervals or asking for more.`, task:`T13`, domain:`Technical`},
    {q:`What is the main purpose of the operation process?`, opts:[`Use the system to deliver its services, with trained operators`,`Restore failed parts so the system can be used again`,`Confirm the built system meets its specified requirements`,`Move the system from its developer to the user's site`], a:0, why:`Operation uses the system to deliver its services: operators, procedures, monitoring and support. Restoring parts is maintenance, and moving it is transition.`, task:`T12`, domain:`Technical`},
    {q:`Who should settle, during design, which faults the ground handles and which wake the crew?`, opts:[`The crew, once on board and familiar with the system`,`The operator alone, as part of its own station rules`,`Supplier and operator together, in an operations concept`,`The safety panel, at its final review just before delivery`], a:2, why:`An operations concept defined during design sets who monitors, commands and decides in each situation, and lets the design support it. The supplier knows how its system fails.`, task:`T12`, domain:`Technical`},
    {q:`Two backup pumps each pass their own test, but both draw through one shared strainer. Which practice finds this?`, opts:[`Retesting each pump under harder conditions than before`,`Reviewing each pump maker's quality records`,`Adding a third pump in parallel with the two`,`Tracing the whole path end to end, operations too`], a:3, why:`Systems thinking looks at the whole path and its interactions. Shared elements only show up when the path is traced end to end, procedures included.`, task:`X1`, domain:`Cross-cutting`},
    {q:`A maintainability decision is made in Development. In which life cycle stages is its effect mostly felt?`, opts:[`Concept and Development`,`Utilization and Support`,`Production and Retirement`,`Development and Production`], a:1, why:`Maintainability is designed in during Development and pays off in Utilization and Support, which run side by side for most of a system's life.`, task:`X1`, domain:`Cross-cutting`},
    {q:`Which model shows how the reliabilities of parts in series and parallel combine into a system value?`, opts:[`An N-squared diagram`,`A state machine diagram`,`A reliability block diagram`,`A functional flow block diagram`], a:2, why:`A reliability block diagram arranges parts in series and parallel so their reliabilities can be combined into a system figure.`, task:`X3`, domain:`Cross-cutting`},
    {q:`Where should a program record how it has tailored an imposed standard, and why?`, opts:[`In its plans, such as the systems engineering management plan`,`In the hazard reports, next to each of the safety controls`,`In the verification matrix, beside each planned test`,`In the minutes of whatever design review comes next`], a:0, why:`Project planning records tailoring decisions and their rationale, usually in the SEMP, so everyone works to the same tailored process.`, task:`M1`, domain:`Technical management`},
    {q:`Reused hardware has flight history only in a different, uncrewed system. How should risk management treat its use on a crewed module?`, opts:[`As no risk at all, since the hardware has already flown`,`As an issue, since the hardware is already designed`,`As the customer's risk, because it accepted the design`,`As a risk, rated on how the new conditions differ`], a:3, why:`Reuse in new conditions is uncertain, so it's identified as a risk and rated on the evidence, including how far the new conditions differ from the old.`, task:`M4`, domain:`Technical management`}
  ],
  closing:(s,all,G)=>{G=G||{};const pk=s.picks||{};const traced=pk[11]===0;const p8=(all&&all[8]&&all[8].picks)||{};const ballast=p8[7]===0?`six hundred kilograms of steel ballast`:`its steel ballast built to the ICD's number`;
    return [
    `Thursday the stand-in rack went into HEARTH. Gus and two technicians walked it up the ramp on the dolly, ${ballast} with Tamarack's real connectors on its back, and slid it into its bay inside the shell while Tiago called the inches. The real rack would come in week twenty seven.`,
    (traced
      ?`Nadia wasn't watching the rack. She'd come in with the drawing folded in her back pocket from the night before, and she went past the rack bay to the forward bulkhead beside the hatch, to see with her hands what she'd found on paper.`
      :`Nadia was inside to watch the connectors mate. The leak check after an install started by shutting the relief line, and Tiago's procedure was clipped to the forward bulkhead beside the hatch, open at step one.`),
    `The relief plate was where the drawings put it, at shoulder height. Two valves side by side, each with its own vent cap and its own bench test tag on a wire. Both sat on one short pipe. The pipe came off one valve upstream of both: a manual valve with a red handle and a tag that said CLOSE FOR PROOF AND LEAK TEST, REOPEN AFTER.`,
    `On the cargo modules there had been one relief valve with this valve in front of it, so the line could be shut for a proof test that ran past the relief setting. At PDR somebody had added the second relief valve for two fault tolerance, on a tee behind the one that was already there. One hole in the shell instead of two. It would have looked like a small change on the day.`+(pk[0]===0?` It was the ninth line on her heritage list, marked low.`:pk[0]===1?` Wes's rule had taken it as built in week twenty two.`:``),
    `She put her hand on the red handle. Leave it shut after a leak test, or let the ball inside it seize, and neither relief valve would ever see the cabin. Three lines on Odette's report. One handle in front of two of them.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"Wes."</span>`,
    `He came in from the rack bay with his head ducked under the hatch frame, and she didn't have to say a thing. He bent and read the tag with his glasses pushed down his nose, then the two valves behind it, then the handle again.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Wes">"Cargo had one valve,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Wes">"One hole in the shell. I was proud of that."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"It's one valve, Wes."</span>`,
    `Back in the bay Tiago hollered the last inch and the rack bumped home, a sound like a file drawer. Wes straightened. He put his hand on the red handle next to hers, and neither of them turned it, or let go.`
  ]},
  episode:{src:`audio/full-ch09.mp3`, len:`about 35 minutes`},
  next:`The Harness`
});
